<article xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article" dtd-version="1.3"><?properties open_access?><processing-meta base-tagset="archiving" mathml-version="3.0" table-model="xhtml" tagset-family="jats"><restricted-by>pmc</restricted-by></processing-meta><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">BMC Cancer</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">BMC Cancer</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>BMC Cancer</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="epub">1471-2407</issn><publisher><publisher-name>BioMed Central</publisher-name><publisher-loc>London</publisher-loc></publisher></journal-meta>
<article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">41316070</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">12661822</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">15209</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/s12885-025-15209-2</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Global burden of respiratory system cancers in 2022 and 2050: incidence and mortality estimates from GLOBOCAN</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>Yuhua</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Yu</surname><given-names>Feng</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Qiu</surname><given-names>Feng</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Fang</surname><given-names>Ke</given-names></name><address><email>fangkeqq88@163.com</email></address><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1">1</xref></contrib><aff id="Aff1"><label>1</label><institution-wrap><institution-id institution-id-type="ROR">https://ror.org/05gbwr869</institution-id><institution-id institution-id-type="GRID">grid.412604.5</institution-id><institution-id institution-id-type="ISNI">0000 0004 1758 4073</institution-id><institution>Department of Oncology, </institution><institution>Gaoxin Branch of the First Affiliated Hospital of Nanchang University, </institution></institution-wrap>No. 7889, Changdong Avenue, Qingshan Lake District, Nanchang City, Jiangxi Province 330095 China </aff><aff id="Aff2"><label>2</label><institution-wrap><institution-id institution-id-type="ROR">https://ror.org/05gbwr869</institution-id><institution-id institution-id-type="GRID">grid.412604.5</institution-id><institution-id institution-id-type="ISNI">0000 0004 1758 4073</institution-id><institution>Department of Oncology, </institution><institution>The First Affiliated Hospital of Nanchang University, </institution></institution-wrap>Nanchang City, Jiangxi Province 330000 China </aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>28</day><month>11</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>28</day><month>11</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="collection"><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>25</volume><elocation-id>1832</elocation-id><history><date date-type="received"><day>17</day><month>7</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>15</day><month>10</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; The Author(s) 2025</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p><bold>Open Access</bold> This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License, which permits any non-commercial use, sharing, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if you modified the licensed material. You do not have permission under this licence to share adapted material derived from this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article&#x02019;s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article&#x02019;s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link>.</license-p></license></permissions><abstract id="Abs1" abstract-type="structured"><sec><title>Background</title><p id="Par1">Respiratory system cancers, primarily encompassing lung cancer, tracheal cancer, bronchial cancer, and laryngeal cancer, severely jeopardize patients&#x02019; lives. Therefore, this study aimed to investigate the global burden of respiratory system cancers in 2022 using the GLOBOCAN 2022 database, to inform global prevention strategies.</p></sec><sec><title>Methods</title><p id="Par2">This study extracted data of respiratory system cancers from GLOBOCAN 2022, stratified into two subsets: laryngeal cancer (C32) and tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (C33, C34). It analyzed the incidence, mortality, and age-standardized rates (ASR) of both subsets across world regions, HDI levels, genders, and age groups, and projected their disease burden from 2022 to 2050.</p></sec><sec><title>Results</title><p id="Par3">For tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (C33, C34), Hungary had the highest age-standardized incidence rates (ASIR) and age-standardized mortality rates (ASMR). For laryngeal cancer alone (C32), India had the highest global ASIR and ASMR. Very high HDI regions had the highest ASR for the combined cancers. Males had higher incidence and mortality than females across regions and HDI strata for both subsets. Incidence and mortality rose markedly after age 40, with faster growth in males. Projection results indicated that by 2050, the male incidence and mortality rates of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers would increase by 87.97% and 94.75%, respectively. For laryngeal cancer, the number of male incident cases and male deaths was expected to grow by 72.53% and 81.33%, respectively.</p></sec><sec><title>Conclusion</title><p id="Par4">This study, grounded in the GLOBOCAN 2022 database, elucidated the respiratory system cancers (lung cancer, tracheal cancer, bronchial cancer, and laryngeal cancer) global burden. It thereby furnished theoretical references for subsequent preventive measures.</p></sec><sec><title>Supplementary Information</title><p>The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12885-025-15209-2.</p></sec></abstract><kwd-group xml:lang="en"><title>Keywords</title><kwd>Respiratory system cancers</kwd><kwd>GLOBOCAN</kwd><kwd>Incidence</kwd><kwd>Mortality</kwd></kwd-group><funding-group><award-group><funding-source><institution>Nanchang City medical and health guiding technology plan project</institution></funding-source><award-id>2023-YLWS-068</award-id><principal-award-recipient><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>Yuhua</given-names></name></principal-award-recipient></award-group></funding-group><funding-group><award-group><funding-source><institution>Nanchang City science and technology support plan project</institution></funding-source><award-id>2022-KJZC-028</award-id><award-id>2022-KJZC-028</award-id><principal-award-recipient><name><surname>Yu</surname><given-names>Feng</given-names></name><name><surname>Qiu</surname><given-names>Feng</given-names></name></principal-award-recipient></award-group></funding-group><funding-group><award-group><funding-source><institution>Professional and Technical Backbone Project of Gaoxin Branch of the First Affiliated Hospital of Nanchang University</institution></funding-source><award-id>GXGG20247201</award-id><principal-award-recipient><name><surname>Fang</surname><given-names>Ke</given-names></name></principal-award-recipient></award-group></funding-group><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>issue-copyright-statement</meta-name><meta-value>&#x000a9; BioMed Central Ltd., part of Springer Nature 2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body><sec id="Sec1"><title>Introduction</title><p id="Par5">Respiratory system cancers are among the most common malignancies worldwide, primarily comprising laryngeal cancer, tracheal cancer, bronchial cancer, and lung cancer [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>]. These cancers originate from different parts of the respiratory system. For example, laryngeal cancer starts from the epithelial tissue of the larynx, tracheal cancer from the tracheal epithelial tissue, bronchial cancer from the epithelial tissue of the main bronchi, and lung cancer from the bronchial mucosa or alveolar tissue [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR2">2</xref>]. Smoking is the primary risk factor for respiratory system cancers [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR3">3</xref>]. The greater the amount of smoking and the longer the smoking duration, the higher the risk of developing these cancers [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR4">4</xref>]. Secondhand smoke also contributes to the disease burden [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR5">5</xref>]. Additionally, outdoor industrial waste gases, automobile exhaust, indoor kitchen fumes, and decoration pollutants, as well as occupational carcinogens like asbestos and radon, significantly increase the probability of getting these cancers [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR6">6</xref>]. Moreover, genetic factors make some populations more susceptible to these cancers [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR7">7</xref>]. The main treatment modalities for respiratory system cancers include surgery, radiotherapy, chemotherapy, molecular targeted therapy, and immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR8">8</xref>]. For early-stage respiratory system cancers, radical surgery or radical radiotherapy is the preferred treatment. For patients in the middle and advanced stages, comprehensive treatment mainly based on drug therapy is adopted. Early diagnosis of respiratory system cancers is challenging, and many patients are already in the middle or advanced stages at the time of diagnosis [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR9">9</xref>]. The goal of treating advanced-stage patients is mainly to improve their symptoms and prolong their survival time, which seriously threatens human health [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR10">10</xref>]. Therefore, it is urgent to strengthen the international research perspective, explore the global disease burden of respiratory system cancers, and provide references for their prevention.</p><p id="Par6">The GLOBOCAN database, maintained by the International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC), serves as a globally authoritative source of cancer statistics. The systematically curated epidemiological data within this database provides essential support for research on global cancer prevention and control [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR11">11</xref>]. Previous studies have employed GLOBOCAN data to analyze respiratory system cancers and other malignancies [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR12">12</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR15">15</xref>]; however, gaps remain in long-term trend forecasting. As key risk factors&#x02014;such as tobacco consumption and air pollution&#x02014;continue to evolve, alongside dynamic shifts in global population demographics and the Human Development Index (HDI), the cumulative effects of these variables may further intensify the burden of respiratory system cancers. Consequently, outdated projections may no longer accurately represent future trends. In this context, updating predictive models with the most recent data is critically important for the precise allocation of public health resources and the development of targeted prevention and treatment strategies&#x02014;particularly for high-risk populations, including residents in low-HDI regions and males.</p><p id="Par7">Given that respiratory system cancers impose a significant burden on health, and there are currently no studies that have used the latest GLOBOCAN 2022 data to conduct a comprehensive assessment and long-term prediction of the global burden of respiratory system cancers.Therefore, this study utilized the GLOBOCAN 2022 database to conduct a comprehensive analysis of respiratory cancer burden, examining prevalence, incidence, and corresponding age-standardized rates (ASR) across different global regions and Human Development Index (HDI) categories. We systematically explored sex-specific and age-specific patterns in both prevalence and incidence rates. Furthermore, projections were made for the evolving disease burden of respiratory cancers from 2022 to 2050 using validated epidemiological models. This not only reveals the scale and trend of future disease burdens, providing a forward-looking basis for the allocation of global public health resources and the formulation of intervention strategies, but also offers crucial evidence-based insights for the targeted prevention and control strategies of global respiratory malignancies.</p></sec><sec id="Sec2"><title>Materials and methods</title><sec id="Sec3"><title>Data source</title><p id="Par8">The data of this study were derived from the GLOBOCAN database, accessible at <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://gco.iarc.who.int/en">https://gco.iarc.who.int/en</ext-link>, which was rolled out by the IARC. This database encompasses data on the occurrence, fatality rates, and trends associated with 36 distinct cancer types across 185 countries and regions worldwide [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR11">11</xref>]. It established a quality control system through hierarchical management, multi-dimensional validation, and dynamic updates. For regions with limited medical infrastructure, incomplete registration, and low Human Development Index (HDI), GLOBOCAN 2022 employed a hierarchical modeling strategy for data processing, specifically including indirect estimation techniques, data imputation, and uncertainty annotation, which ensured effective integration and reasonable estimation of global data on respiratory system cancers. Based on the 10th Revision of the International Classification of Diseases (ICD) (ICD-10), the study extracted data for two separately recorded subsets of respiratory system cancers from the GLOBOCAN 2022 database:</p><p id="Par9">Laryngeal cancer: Corresponding to ICD-10 code C32; Tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers: Corresponding to ICD-10 codes C33 (tracheal cancer) and C34 (bronchial and lung cancer). Data for both subsets were retrieved from 185 countries and regions, and each subset was segregated by gender and 18 age groups (with 5-year intervals as the cut-off points: 0&#x02013;4, 5&#x02013;9, &#x02026;, 80&#x02013;84, and 85 and above). All data are available via the GLOBOCAN database.</p></sec><sec id="Sec4"><title>Demographic characteristics</title><p id="Par10">The Human Development Index (HDI) is a comprehensive measurement indicator that aims to comprehensively and scientifically reflect the development level of a country or region. It is evaluated based on three fundamental variables: the Gross National Income (GNI) per capita, education&#x02019;s level, and the life expectancy. Grounded on the Human Development Report of the United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) and the data classification criteria of the GLOBOCAN 2022 database, different countries and regions are classified into six HDI levels: Very high HDI country, High HDI country (excluding China), Medium HDI country (excluding India), Low HDI country, China, and India [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">16</xref>].</p></sec><sec id="Sec5"><title>Statistical analysis</title><p id="Par11">The global burden of respiratory system cancers was assessed via incidence rates, mortality rates, and age-standardized rates (per 100,000 person-years), with age-standardized incidence rates (ASIR) and age-standardized mortality rates (ASMR) calculated using the direct age-standardization method based on the 1966 Segi-Doll World standard population as the reference. The &#x0201c;ggplot2&#x0201d; package (v 3.4.1) [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR17">17</xref>] was harnessed to diagram maps of different world regions and different HDI regions in 2022, respectively, to visualize the burden of respiratory system cancers in 2022. Meanwhile, plots were diagrammed via the &#x0201c;ggplot2&#x0201d; package (v 3.4.1) to present respiratory system cancers&#x02019; incidence and mortality rates in 2022 for different genders, categorized by world regions and the four levels of the HDI. Besides, the &#x0201c;ggalt&#x0201d; package (v 3.5.2) (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org">https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org</ext-link>) was also deployed to diagram graphs showing the results of respiratory system cancers&#x02019; incidence and mortality rates for different age groups. In addition, to predict the incidence and mortality rates of the two respiratory system cancer subsets (laryngeal cancer; tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers) from 2022 to 2050, the &#x0201c;Cancer Tomorrow&#x0201d; from the GLOBOCAN database was used for the prediction. This tool employed a stratified time series regression model, which was based on the incidence and mortality data of respiratory system cancers from the 2022 GLOBOCAN database. By integrating historical retrospective data, it identified long-term trends in disease burden across different genders and HDI groups. Furthermore, the fitted disease trends were coupled with population projection data from the United Nations, based on which the incidence and mortality numbers for time points such as 2026, 2030, and up to 2050 were derived. The core assumptions of this prediction model included the stability of disease trends (the incidence/mortality risk trends remained stable from 2022 to 2050, with no abrupt changes caused by sudden interventions), population structure-driven effects (the increasing proportion of elderly populations was a core driver of changes in disease burden), and the rationality of data extrapolation (low HDI countries referenced trends from countries within the same region and with the same HDI level that had complete data). The findings were presented by the &#x0201c;ggplot2&#x0201d; package (v 3.4.1) for visualization. The R software (v 4.2.2) was harnessed to statistically analyze all of the data.</p></sec></sec><sec id="Sec6"><title>Results</title><sec id="Sec7"><title>Incidence and mortality rates of respiratory system cancers by world region and six levels of HDI</title><p id="Par12">This section analyzes two subsets of respiratory system cancers: (1) tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD-10: C33, C34); (2) laryngeal cancer (ICD-10: C32), In 2022, for tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers combined (ICD-10: C33, C34), Hungary had the highest ASIR of respiratory system cancers, which was 47.57 cases per 100,000 people. New Caledonia ranked second, with an ASIR of 41.77 cases per 100,000 people. China followed, with an ASIR of 40.78 cases per 100,000 people (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig1" ref-type="fig">1</xref>a, Supplementary Table 1). In 2022, the country with the highest ASMR of respiratory system cancers was also Hungary, with 39.8 cases per 100,000 people. Turkey ranked second, with 35.06 cases per 100,000 people, and French Polynesia came in third, having 34.95 cases per 100,000 people (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig1" ref-type="fig">1</xref>b, Supplementary Table 2). For laryngeal cancer alone (ICD-10: C32), India had the highest global ASIR (3.61 cases per 100,000 people) and ASMR (1.60 cases per 100,000 people), showing a prominent disease burden in South Asia (Supplementary Figure S1a-b, Supplementary Tables 3&#x02013;4). For tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers combined (ICD-10: C33, C34), the comparative analysis across six HDI tiers revealed that very high HDI regions had the highest ASIR (28.65 per 100,000 population) and ASMR (19.14 per 100,000 population), while low HDI regions had the lowest (ASIR 3.58, ASMR 3.29 per 100,000 population) (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig1" ref-type="fig">1</xref>c-d, Supplementary Table 5); For laryngeal cancer alone (ICD-10: C32), India had the highest age-standardized incidence rate (ASIR, 2.54 cases per 100,000 population) and age-standardized mortality rate (ASMR, 1.6 cases per 100,000 population). Medium HDI regions (excluding India) followed closely with an ASIR of 2.27 cases per 100,000 population and an ASMR of 1.45 cases per 100,000 population. China had the lowest ASIR (1.16 cases per 100,000 population) and ASMR (0.62 cases per 100,000 population), with distinct disparities across different HDI regions. (Figure S1c-d, Supplementary Tables 6&#x02013;7). The observed disparities in respiratory system cancer burden&#x02014;both for the combined subsets (tracheal, bronchial, lung cancers) and laryngeal cancer alone&#x02014;across nations and six HDI tiers likely reflected multifactorial influences. These included differential exposure to tobacco smoke (including chewing tobacco linked to laryngeal cancer) and ambient air pollutants, varying implementation of early detection programs, and disparities in access to specialized oncology care.</p><p id="Par13">
<fig id="Fig1"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>Distribution of incidence and mortality of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD-10: C33, C34) by world region and six HDI levels in 2022.<bold> a</bold>&#x000a0;ASIR of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD-10: C33, C34) in 185 countries, 2022. <bold>b</bold>&#x000a0;ASMR of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD-10: C33, C34) in 185 countries, 2022. <bold>c</bold>&#x000a0;Incidence of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD-10: C33, C34) across regions with different HDI levels, 2022. <bold>d</bold>&#x000a0;Mortality of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD-10: C33, C34) across regions with different HDI levels, 2022</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="12885_2025_15209_Fig1_HTML" id="d33e346"/></fig>
</p></sec><sec id="Sec8"><title>Sex-specific differences in respiratory system cancers incidence and mortality by world region and HDI</title><p id="Par14">An analysis was executed on respiratory system cancers&#x02019; incidence and mortality rates of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD-10: C33, C34) among males and females across different global regions and HDI categories. The results evidenced that in Latin America and the Caribbean, Europe, Asia, and Africa, respiratory system cancers&#x02019; incidence and mortality rates among males were higher than those among females (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig2" ref-type="fig">2</xref>a). Meanwhile, in each HDI region, the incidence and mortality rates among males were also higher than those among females (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig2" ref-type="fig">2</xref>b).</p><p id="Par15">A parallel analysis of laryngeal cancer alone (ICD-10: C32) confirmed a consistent male-dominant pattern: in Latin America and the Caribbean, Europe, and Asia, male laryngeal cancer incidence (e.g., 3.78 vs. 0.65 per 100,000 in Latin America) and mortality (e.g., 2.40 vs. 0.21 per 100,000 in Europe) were substantially higher than those in females (Figure S2a). Across all four HDI tiers, males also exhibited higher laryngeal cancer ASIR and ASMR than females, with the gap most pronounced in medium HDI regions (male ASIR 4.39 vs. female 0.60 per 100,000) (Figure S2b).</p><p id="Par16">The distinct male-dominant pattern across regions and HDI categories indicates a need for customized interventions. Such initiatives should focus on male-targeted risk reduction and improved healthcare access for respiratory cancer control.</p><p id="Par17">
<fig id="Fig2"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>Incidence and mortality of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD-10: C33, C34) by sex in 2022: world region and four HDI levels. <bold>a</bold>&#x000a0;Sex-specific differences in incidence and mortality of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD-10: C33, C34) by world region in 2022. <bold>b</bold>&#x000a0;Sex-specific differences in incidence and mortality of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD-10: C33, C34) by four HDI levels in 2022</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="12885_2025_15209_Fig2_HTML" id="d33e377"/></fig>
</p></sec><sec id="Sec9"><title>Age-related progression and gender discrepancies in respiratory system cancers incidence and mortality</title><p id="Par18">For tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD-10: C33, C34), incidence and mortality rates remained relatively low without noteworthy increases in the 0&#x02013;39 age group but began to rise from 40 years onward, demonstrating progressively steeper increases with advancing age. Notably, from the 40&#x02013;44 age group onward, males consistently exhibited higher incidence and mortality rates than females, with a more rapid rate of increase observed in males (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig3" ref-type="fig">3</xref>). These findings emphasize the need for age and gender targeted screening and preventive measures, starting from the age of 40, to mitigate the escalating burden of respiratory system cancers. Due to limitations in the availability of data in the GLOBOCAN database, ASIR and ASMR data for laryngeal cancer by gender and age in 2022 are not presented. Please refer to the discussion section for further details on this limitation and possible future research directions.</p><p id="Par19">
<fig id="Fig3"><label>Fig. 3</label><caption><p>Age-related progression and gender discrepancies in incidence and mortality of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD-10: C33, C34) (2022)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="12885_2025_15209_Fig3_HTML" id="d33e394"/></fig>
</p></sec><sec id="Sec10"><title>Prediction of the burden of tracheal, bronchial, lung, and laryngeal cancers in 2022&#x02013;2050</title><p id="Par20">It was predicted that the number of incident and fatal cases of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD-10: C33, C34) would show an upward trend year-by-year from 2022 to 2050. It was projected that by 2050, the number of male incident cases of respiratory system cancers would increase from 1.6 to 2.95&#x000a0;million, with an expected growth of 87.97%, and the number of deaths would amount to 2.40&#x000a0;million, with an expected growth of 94.75% (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig4" ref-type="fig">4</xref>a, Supplementary Table 8). Projected estimates suggested that female respiratory system cancer cases would rise from 908,630 to 1.67&#x000a0;million globally by 2050, with an expected growth of 83.26%, and the corresponding number of female deaths would also increase from 0.6 to 1.15&#x000a0;million, with an expected growth of 96.17% (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig4" ref-type="fig">4</xref>b, Supplementary Table 8).</p><p id="Par21">A parallel prediction for laryngeal cancer alone (ICD-10: C32) revealed a consistent upward trend: Global male incident cases of laryngeal cancer were projected to increase from 165,794 to 286,039 by 2050, with an expected growth of 72.53%, and male deaths would rise from 90,384 to 163,895, with an expected growth of 81.33%. For females, laryngeal cancer incident cases were expected to grow from 23,397 to 39,726 by 2050, with an expected growth of 69.79%, and the number of female deaths would increase from 12,975 to 23,786, with an expected growth of 83.32% (Figure S3a-b, Supplementary Table 9).</p><p id="Par22">Overall, it was predicted that by 2050, both the number of male patients with respiratory system cancers and the number of male deaths from such cancers would be far higher than those of females. The projection not only sounds the alarm for global prevention and control of respiratory system cancers, especially among men, but also provides crucial data guidance for the forward-looking allocation of public health resources.</p><p id="Par23">
<fig id="Fig4"><label>Fig. 4</label><caption><p>Prediction of incidence and mortality of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD &#x02212;&#x02009;10: C33, C34) from 2022 to 2050. <bold>a</bold>&#x000a0;Prediction of incidence of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD &#x02212;&#x02009;10: C33, C34) by sex, 2022&#x02013;2050. <bold>b</bold>&#x000a0;Prediction of mortality of tracheal, bronchial, and lung cancers (ICD &#x02212;&#x02009;10: C33, C34) by sex, 2022&#x02013;2050</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="12885_2025_15209_Fig4_HTML" id="d33e425"/></fig>
</p></sec></sec><sec id="Sec11"><title>Discussion</title><p id="Par24">Respiratory system cancers remain the most prevalent and lethal malignant tumors worldwide, posing a severe threat to human life and health [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR18">18</xref>]. They also represent the most burdensome type of cancer [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR19">19</xref>]. Comprehensive assessments of the global disease burden of respiratory system cancers are still insufficient, and there is a lack of the latest research results based on GLOBOCAN.</p><p id="Par25">This study analyzed the incidence, mortality, and corresponding ASR of respiratory system cancers in 2022 across different world regions, HDI regions, different genders, and age groups, leveraging data from the GLOBOCAN 2022 database. And using the GLOBOCAN 2022 database, the future burden of respiratory system cancers was predicted up to the year 2050. This will help to optimize the existing prevention and treatment measures and formulate different prevention and treatment plans for different regions, genders, and age groups [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR20">20</xref>]. Eventually, it will improve the survival rate and quality of life of patients with respiratory system cancers.</p><p id="Par26">The findings of this study are highly consistent with previous global epidemiological research on respiratory system cancers, collectively revealing significant regional disparities in disease burden and their underlying influencing factors. The 2022 data show that Hungary ranked first globally in both age-standardized incidence rate (ASIR) and mortality rate (ASMR) for respiratory system cancers, followed by New Caledonia and China. This geographical distribution closely correlates with the disease burden characteristics observed in regions with very high Human Development Index (HDI). Notably, the ASIR in very high HDI regions was significantly higher than in other areas, reaching 7&#x02013;8 times that of low HDI regions. This magnitude of difference aligns with the global epidemiological features of lung cancer reported by Chao Li et al. [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR21">21</xref>]. and the gradient variations in subtype-specific incidence rates across HDI levels [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR22">22</xref>].</p><p id="Par27">This disparity may stem from the combined effects of multiple factors. First, developed countries in Europe and America reached peak smoking prevalence as early as the mid-20th century, and the cumulative exposure effects have led to high incidence rates among their aging populations today. Second, significant regional variations exist in the distribution of pathological subtypes&#x02014;for instance, the ASIR for lung adenocarcinoma among males in East Asia was 23.5 per 100,000, while the ASIR for squamous cell carcinoma among males in Central and Eastern Europe was 17.5 per 100,000 [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR23">23</xref>]. Additionally, differences in healthcare resource accessibility and diagnostic and treatment standards cannot be overlooked. For example, regions like India have begun emphasizing the application of molecular testing for EGFR and ALK in precision therapy [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR24">24</xref>]. It is worth noting that the data from this study also reveal that within countries with a high HDI, the disease burden is not uniform. Even within the high HDI category, there is significant heterogeneity in the incidence rates, pathological subtypes composition, and diagnostic and treatment levels across different regions. These findings suggest that when formulating prevention and control strategies for respiratory system cancers, it is essential to comprehensively consider region-specific risk factors (e.g., tobacco control policies, air pollution management) and the allocation of medical resources (e.g., early screening programs, accessibility to targeted therapies) to achieve precise disease burden mitigation.</p><p id="Par28">Our further analysis of respiratory cancer subtypes reveals that laryngeal cancer exhibits distinct regional clustering patterns. India demonstrates the highest global age-standardized incidence rate (ASIR) and age-standardized mortality rate (ASMR) for laryngeal cancer, while medium Human Development Index (HDI) countries (excluding India) also maintain elevated incidence levels. This distribution pattern not only confirms the heterogeneity of respiratory cancer burden across regions with varying development levels but also suggests that laryngeal cancer prevention strategies must transcend conventional risk factor frameworks (e.g., smoking and industrialization) to focus on the synergistic effects of region-specific risk factors. Within the Indian population, the etiological profile of laryngeal cancer is closely associated with interactions among multiple risk factors. Numerous studies have established smoking and alcohol consumption as core pathogenic factors [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR25">25</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR28">28</xref>]. Concurrently, India-specific cultural and dietary habits&#x02014;such as betel quid chewing, inadequate fruit and vegetable intake, and preference for spicy fried foods&#x02014;further elevate disease risk [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR27">27</xref>]. Consequently, India&#x02019;s exceptionally high laryngeal cancer burden likely results from the combined effects of traditional risk factors (smoking, alcohol) and regional exposures (chewing behaviors, nutritional imbalances), with similar risk factor combinations potentially explaining the high incidence rates observed in certain medium-HDI countries. From a clinical intervention perspective, effective laryngeal cancer management requires precise initial evaluation and personalized treatment plans developed by multidisciplinary teams (MDT), with the dual objectives of achieving oncological cure while preserving laryngeal function to maintain patients&#x02019; quality of life [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR25">25</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR29">29</xref>]. For early-stage laryngeal cancer, both transoral laser microsurgery and radiotherapy serve as radical treatment modalities with comparable local control rates [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR29">29</xref>]. However, in India and selected medium-HDI countries, disparities in healthcare resource distribution&#x02014;leading to limited treatment accessibility and variations in care quality&#x02014;may constitute critical barriers exacerbating laryngeal cancer mortality, thereby highlighting key targets for regionalized prevention and control strategies.</p><p id="Par29">This study identifies significant regional and gender-based disparities in the global burden of respiratory system cancers, stratified by Human Development Index (HDI) levels. The findings demonstrate that in Latin America and the Caribbean, Europe, Asia, and Africa, both the incidence and mortality rates of respiratory cancers are consistently higher among males compared to females, with this disparity observed across all HDI strata. These gender differences may result from multiple interrelated factors. First, males are more frequently exposed to well-established risk factors, including occupational carcinogens (e.g., asbestos, silica), active and passive tobacco smoke exposure, and environmental pollutants [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR30">30</xref>]. Second, notable pathological distinctions exist between the sexes: males predominantly develop more aggressive histological subtypes, such as squamous cell carcinoma and small cell lung cancer, whereas females exhibit a higher prevalence of adenocarcinoma [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR31">31</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR32">32</xref>]. This biological variation carries important clinical implications, as adenocarcinomas are more amenable to early detection, and advanced-stage patients tend to respond more favorably to molecularly targeted therapies and immunotherapeutic interventions [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR33">33</xref>]. Furthermore, occupational gender segregation contributes to the disparity&#x02014;males are disproportionately represented in high-risk sectors such as construction and mining, thereby increasing their exposure to physical and chemical carcinogens [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR34">34</xref>]. It is noteworthy that laryngeal cancer, as a key subtype of respiratory system malignancies, demonstrates distinct gender heterogeneity that extends beyond the general trends outlined above. Although the incidence rate in males is substantially higher than in females, evidence suggests that women may exhibit greater susceptibility to the carcinogenic effects of tobacco and alcohol. Furthermore, hormonal factors&#x02014;such as estrogen&#x02014;may influence both disease vulnerability and treatment outcomes [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR26">26</xref>]. Of particular concern is that female patients may occasionally receive suboptimal treatment, underscoring the importance of integrating gender-specific considerations into clinical practice to promote equitable care [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR26">26</xref>].</p><p id="Par30">These findings underscore the necessity of implementing gender-specific prevention and control strategies. For male-dominated high-risk occupations, enhanced occupational health and safety measures, along with tailored smoking cessation initiatives, should be prioritized. Meanwhile, the relatively favorable prognosis associated with female-predominant adenocarcinoma highlights the critical role of molecular profiling in guiding early detection and precision medicine approaches.</p><p id="Par31">This study demonstrates that the epidemiology of respiratory system tumors exhibits a pronounced age-related pattern, with both incidence and mortality rates increasing markedly after the age of 40. This pivotal age threshold is associated with multiple pathophysiological and environmental exposure factors. By this age, smokers typically have accumulated 15&#x02013;20 pack-years of tobacco exposure, resulting in irreversible DNA damage to bronchial epithelial cells [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR35">35</xref>]. Concurrently, the cumulative deposition of environmental pollutants such as PM2.5 in the lungs increases exponentially with age, contributing to chronic inflammation and genetic mutations. Furthermore, the age of 40 represents the peak period for the clinical manifestation of occupational carcinogens such as asbestos and arsenic, which have latency periods of 15&#x02013;30 years, particularly affecting construction and mining workers [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR36">36</xref>]. These converging risk factors underscore the necessity of targeted preventive measures and early screening among individuals aged 40 or older with a history of smoking or occupational exposure. Laryngeal cancer deviates from this age distribution pattern in specific subgroups of the population. For young patients under 40 years old without a history of smoking or alcohol consumption, the cause of their laryngeal cancer may have a particularity - it is more likely to be related to unique molecular or genetic abnormalities rather than traditional environmental exposure factors [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR37">37</xref>]. Such patients should be classified into a unique subgroup and advanced technologies such as gene sequencing should be used to explore their pathogenesis in order to achieve truly individualized treatment [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR37">37</xref>]. Predictive models estimate an additional 2.95 million male cases and 2.4 million deaths by 2050 [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR38">38</xref>], highlighting the urgent need for evidence-based interventions. While tobacco control and environmental risk management remain fundamental preventive strategies, this study strongly advocates for the integration of low-dose CT screening into high-risk population management, as it has been shown to significantly enhance early tumor detection and improve survival outcomes [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR39">39</xref>,&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR40">40</xref>]. A comprehensive prevention strategy that combines population-level risk factor regulation with individualized early detection represents the most promising approach to mitigating the growing global burden of respiratory system tumors.</p><p id="Par32">Air pollution, as a key environmental risk factor for respiratory system cancers, is becoming increasingly prominent in low- and middle-income countries as well as in rapidly industrializing regions (such as Asia and Africa), and has become a significant public health challenge that cannot be ignored. Multiple epidemiological studies [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR41">41</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR43">43</xref>] have clearly demonstrated that pollutants such as outdoor industrial exhaust, vehicle exhaust, indoor kitchen fumes, and PM2.5, nitrogen dioxide (NO&#x02082;), and ozone (O&#x02083;) released by decorative materials, when entering the human body through the respiratory system, can trigger chronic inflammatory responses and oxidative stress damage, thereby significantly increasing the risk of cancer in respiratory mucosal cells.Among them, PM2.5 acts as the core carrier, capable of adsorbing polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs), heavy metals and other strong carcinogenic substances deep into the alveolar tissues, directly damaging the DNA structure and inhibiting its repair function [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR41">41</xref>]. This mechanism is particularly prominent in countries with accelerated industrialization processes such as China and India. The high incidence of respiratory system cancers in these countries is significantly associated with long-term exposure to high concentrations of PM2.5 [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR44">44</xref>]. This mechanism is particularly prominent in countries with accelerated industrialization processes such as China and India. The high incidence of respiratory system cancers in these countries is significantly associated with long-term exposure to high concentrations of PM2.5 [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR44">44</xref>]. Although data for the African region is insufficient, the severe PM2.5 phenomena that have emerged during the urbanization process in countries like Ghana and Nigeria [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR45">45</xref>] already indicate that this region faces similar cancer risks. It is worth noting that the carcinogenic effect of air pollution has a population-wide characteristic. Even in non-smoking groups, long-term exposure to polluted environments still constitutes an important cause of respiratory cancer [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR46">46</xref>,&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR47">47</xref>]. Therefore, the future prevention and control strategies for respiratory system cancers should, on the basis of continuously strengthening smoking control measures, focus on strengthening the construction of the air quality monitoring system and pollution source governance. In particular, early screening programs should be implemented and public health education should be carried out in high-pollution areas. Through multi-dimensional intervention measures, the burden of cancer can be effectively controlled. Simultaneously, to alleviate the global burden of laryngeal cancer, particularly in high-burden regions, multi-level and targeted measures should be integrated into the aforementioned framework. At the public health level, strict control of tobacco (including cigarettes and bidis) and alcohol consumption must be reinforced. Additionally, in specific regions such as India, health education programs should be implemented to address local habits like betel nut chewing. At the clinical level, the promotion of standardized diagnosis and treatment, led by multidisciplinary teams (MDTs), is essential. This approach should balance treatment efficacy with the patient&#x02019;s quality of life, while also catering to the specific needs of special groups, such as female patients and young non-smokers. Looking ahead, future research endeavors should focus on elucidating the mechanisms of regional risk factors, exploring the biological underpinnings of gender differences, and identifying patient subgroups at the molecular level. These efforts will lay a solid foundation for the development of more effective preventive strategies and precise treatment modalities.</p><p id="Par33">This study presents a comprehensive evaluation of the global burden of respiratory system tumors, utilizing the most recent GLOBOCAN 2022 data. Key findings demonstrate substantial regional disparities in disease distribution and project a concerning upward trend in incidence by 2050, with high Human Development Index (HDI) regions and male populations experiencing disproportionately higher disease burdens. Although these findings provide valuable insights for formulating targeted prevention strategies, some limitations must be acknowledged. Firstly, the completeness of global cancer registration systems varies significantly. In low-resource areas, due to insufficient reporting and inconsistent registration practices, the data mostly rely on extrapolation based on smoking rates and other risk factors, which can easily lead to biases due to the lack of long-term registration and other basic data; in high-resource countries, although they mainly use measured data, there is still a problem of underreporting of early asymptomatic cases. Furthermore, we encountered difficulties in acquiring the age - standardized incidence rate (ASIR) and age - standardized mortality rate (ASMR) data for laryngeal cancer in 2022, disaggregated by gender and age. Such circumstances have the potential to introduce biases in data reporting. Secondly, when using age-standardized rates for analysis, if the population age structure of the research subjects is significantly different, it may mask the true characteristics of the actual disease burden. Moreover, the GLOBOCAN 2022 database only provides data on incidence, mortality, and demographic information at the national/regional level, lacking individual risk factor information, and cannot accurately assess the specific impact of smoking pack-years, PM 2.5 exposure, etc. on the burden of respiratory cancer. To address these limitations, future research should prioritize improving the coverage and data quality of cancer registration systems in low-resource areas, conducting evaluations of intervention effects in high-risk populations; optimizing the interpretation of estimates in low-resource areas based on regional-specific risk factors, strengthening the construction of the global cancer registration network; collecting individual-level data to precisely control variables, using Bootstrap resampling to supplement confidence interval analysis and improve sensitivity tests, and enhancing the reliability of conclusions; and establishing a transnational collaboration mechanism centered on WHO, IARC, and UNDP, unifying registration standards, conducting cross-regional studies, and coordinating resource allocation, to provide support for precise prevention and control.</p></sec><sec id="Sec12" sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supplementary Information</title><p>
